1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,711
Hello, I am Suraj Patel, USN 24BTRBT029, from B.Tech Computer Science with

2
00:00:05,761 --> 00:00:11,398
specialisation in Blockchain Technology, Semester five, at Jain University.

3
00:00:11,448 --> 00:00:17,160
My Corda Development mini project is topic twenty five: Implementation of an

4
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:21,027
External REST API Integration with a Corda CorDapp.

5
00:00:21,077 --> 00:00:25,652
I built an invoice application on Corda 4.12 and put a Spring

6
00:00:25,702 --> 00:00:29,898
Boot REST API in front of it, so outside systems like an

7
00:00:29,948 --> 00:00:34,372
ERP or a web app can use the ledger with simple JSON calls.

8
00:00:35,122 --> 00:00:39,778
When two companies trade, the seller and the buyer each keep their

9
00:00:39,828 --> 00:00:43,558
own copy of the same invoice in separate ERP systems.

10
00:00:43,608 --> 00:00:48,692
These copies drift apart, and finance teams waste days reconciling them.

11
00:00:48,742 --> 00:00:53,185
Corda fixes this with one shared, signed and notarised invoice.

12
00:00:53,235 --> 00:00:57,820
But a Corda node is only reachable through Corda RPC, which needs

13
00:00:57,870 --> 00:01:01,171
Java libraries and Corda classes on the client.

14
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:05,379
So my problem was to build a secure REST bridge that normal

15
00:01:05,429 --> 00:01:07,304
business software can call.

16
00:01:08,054 --> 00:01:09,653
I set five objectives.

17
00:01:09,703 --> 00:01:14,898
First, model the invoice as a linear state with four statuses: issued,

18
00:01:14,948 --> 00:01:16,996
approved, paid and rejected.

19
00:01:17,046 --> 00:01:21,117
Second, enforce the rules in a contract and four flows.

20
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:25,838
Third, the core of the topic, build a production style REST API

21
00:01:25,888 --> 00:01:29,884
with authentication, validation and clear error codes.

22
00:01:29,934 --> 00:01:35,954
Fourth, prove it works from outside, using a Python ERP client, Postman and curl.

23
00:01:36,004 --> 00:01:41,124
And fifth, test everything automatically and measure the API latency.

24
00:01:41,874 --> 00:01:43,919
Why REST in front of Corda?

25
00:01:43,969 --> 00:01:47,799
Corda RPC is designed for Java and Kotlin clients.

26
00:01:47,849 --> 00:01:52,766
It needs the RPC library and the CorDapp classes, it talks AMQP,

27
00:01:52,816 --> 00:01:55,560
and it returns Corda specific types.

28
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:59,673
A browser cannot use it, and an SAP or dot net system

29
00:01:59,723 --> 00:02:01,846
cannot easily use it either.

30
00:02:01,896 --> 00:02:08,364
REST works from any language, JSON is universal, and Swagger documents the contract.

31
00:02:08,414 --> 00:02:13,098
So I kept RPC inside the trusted server and exposed only REST

32
00:02:13,148 --> 00:02:14,728
to the outside world.

33
00:02:15,478 --> 00:02:18,046
These are the Corda concepts I used.

34
00:02:18,096 --> 00:02:22,991
The invoice is a LinearState, so every version keeps the same linear

35
00:02:23,041 --> 00:02:25,536
ID, which the API uses in its URLs.

36
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:30,626
Following the UTXO model, each approve, pay or reject consumes the old

37
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:34,990
state and creates a new one, which gives a free audit trail.

38
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:40,516
The contract runs on every node, flows collect signatures, a notary prevents

39
00:02:40,566 --> 00:02:44,516
double spends, and RPC is the only door into the vault.

40
00:02:45,266 --> 00:02:47,282
Here is the architecture.

41
00:02:47,332 --> 00:02:52,818
On the left are the external systems: my Python ERP client, Postman

42
00:02:52,868 --> 00:02:55,132
or curl, and web dashboards.

43
00:02:55,182 --> 00:02:57,610
They only speak HTTP and JSON.

44
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:02,486
In the middle, I run one Spring Boot instance per node, the

45
00:03:02,536 --> 00:03:07,195
Seller API on port 10050 and the Buyer API on port 10060.

46
00:03:07,245 --> 00:03:13,062
Each request passes logging and API key filters, the controller and the

47
00:03:13,112 --> 00:03:17,194
service, which calls the node through CordaRPCOps.

48
00:03:17,244 --> 00:03:20,664
On the right, the nodes talk peer to peer.

49
00:03:21,414 --> 00:03:23,244
This is the ledger design.

50
00:03:23,294 --> 00:03:28,958
The InvoiceState holds the linear ID, invoice number, seller and buyer, amount,

51
00:03:29,008 --> 00:03:35,395
currency, description, due date, status and remarks, and both companies are participants.

52
00:03:35,445 --> 00:03:40,603
Issue needs an amount above zero, different parties and both signatures.

53
00:03:40,653 --> 00:03:45,738
Approve, pay and reject need only the buyer's signature and must follow

54
00:03:45,788 --> 00:03:47,691
the lifecycle on the right.

55
00:03:47,741 --> 00:03:53,260
On every transition, the invoice number, amount and parties can never change.

56
00:03:54,010 --> 00:03:56,447
This sequence shows a new invoice.

57
00:03:56,497 --> 00:04:01,860
The API checks the key and validates the body, then calls startFlowDynamic

58
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:03,249
on the seller node.

59
00:04:03,299 --> 00:04:08,809
IssueInvoiceFlow builds the transaction, verifies the contract and signs it.

60
00:04:08,859 --> 00:04:14,294
Through CollectSignaturesFlow, the buyer checks the proposal and signs too.

61
00:04:14,344 --> 00:04:20,292
FinalityFlow gets the notary signature and records the transaction in both vaults.

62
00:04:20,342 --> 00:04:24,023
The API then returns 201 with the transaction hash.

63
00:04:24,073 --> 00:04:27,680
Approve, pay and reject share one buyer side flow.

64
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:30,671
The API has eleven endpoints.

65
00:04:30,721 --> 00:04:36,200
Four describe the node: me, peers, notaries and a public health check.

66
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:40,939
POST on the invoices path issues an invoice and returns 201.

67
00:04:40,989 --> 00:04:47,180
GET lists invoices, optionally filtered by status, or fetches one by linear ID.

68
00:04:47,230 --> 00:04:52,314
PUT approve, pay and reject start the matching flows on the buyer

69
00:04:52,364 --> 00:04:55,474
node, and history returns every version.

70
00:04:55,524 --> 00:05:00,924
Every response uses one envelope: success, data, error and timestamp.

71
00:05:01,674 --> 00:05:06,039
Because this API is the front door to a ledger, I added

72
00:05:06,089 --> 00:05:08,286
production style protection.

73
00:05:08,336 --> 00:05:14,707
Every call except health needs an X-API-KEY header, otherwise the answer is 401.

74
00:05:14,757 --> 00:05:20,326
Bean validation rejects a negative amount or a wrong currency with 400

75
00:05:20,376 --> 00:05:22,654
and a clear list of problems.

76
00:05:22,704 --> 00:05:27,148
A global handler maps an unknown linear ID to 404, and a

77
00:05:27,198 --> 00:05:29,877
flow or contract rejection to 422.

78
00:05:29,927 --> 00:05:33,088
I also enabled CORS and request logging.

79
00:05:33,838 --> 00:05:38,978
My tech stack is Corda 4.12 Community Edition on Java 17, starting

80
00:05:39,028 --> 00:05:43,775
from the official R3 Java template and built with Gradle 7.6.

81
00:05:43,825 --> 00:05:49,122
The REST layer uses Spring Boot 3.2 with Spring Web, Bean Validation

82
00:05:49,172 --> 00:05:51,167
and springdoc for Swagger.

83
00:05:51,217 --> 00:05:57,930
For testing I used the Corda ledger DSL, MockNetwork, and Spring MockMvc with Mockito.

84
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:03,041
The ERP simulation is written in Python with requests, and I also

85
00:06:03,091 --> 00:06:05,636
provide Postman and curl scripts.

86
00:06:06,386 --> 00:06:07,546
Now the results.

87
00:06:07,596 --> 00:06:11,783
I wrote forty two automated tests, and all of them pass.

88
00:06:11,833 --> 00:06:16,928
Fifteen contract tests use the ledger DSL to cover valid and invalid

89
00:06:16,978 --> 00:06:22,451
cases, like a zero amount, a missing buyer signature or a changed amount.

90
00:06:22,501 --> 00:06:26,687
Six flow tests run on an in memory network with a notary

91
00:06:26,737 --> 00:06:28,125
and both companies.

92
00:06:28,175 --> 00:06:33,043
And twenty one API tests use MockMvc with a mocked CordaRPCOps to

93
00:06:33,093 --> 00:06:37,128
check routing, security, validation and error mapping.

94
00:06:37,878 --> 00:06:44,172
For the live demo, I deployed the notary, SellerCorp and BuyerCorp, started

95
00:06:44,222 --> 00:06:47,302
them headless, and started both APIs.

96
00:06:47,352 --> 00:06:51,447
On the left is the Swagger UI for the seller API.

97
00:06:51,497 --> 00:06:57,115
On the right, my ERP client has issued three invoices, approved and

98
00:06:57,165 --> 00:07:01,345
paid one, approved another and rejected the third.

99
00:07:01,395 --> 00:07:05,997
It also showed every error case: 401, 400, 422 and 404.

100
00:07:06,047 --> 00:07:10,396
Finally, both vaults held exactly the same invoices.

101
00:07:11,146 --> 00:07:17,259
For performance, I timed twenty POST calls to the invoices endpoint after

102
00:07:17,309 --> 00:07:18,779
two warm up calls.

103
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:25,364
Each call runs the whole flow, including the buyer signature and notarisation.

104
00:07:25,414 --> 00:07:31,274
The average was 583 milliseconds, the fastest 391 and the slowest 766.

105
00:07:31,324 --> 00:07:35,158
A simple read took only about 78 milliseconds.

106
00:07:35,208 --> 00:07:40,308
So the REST layer adds very little; almost all the write time

107
00:07:40,358 --> 00:07:41,912
is Corda consensus.

108
00:07:41,962 --> 00:07:44,360
Everything ran on one laptop.

109
00:07:45,110 --> 00:07:49,514
The big advantage is reach: any system that can send HTTP can

110
00:07:49,564 --> 00:07:52,726
now use the ledger, without Corda libraries.

111
00:07:52,776 --> 00:07:57,472
The API is documented, validated and secured at the edge, and the

112
00:07:57,522 --> 00:07:59,662
ledger guarantees stay intact.

113
00:07:59,712 --> 00:08:03,824
On the other hand, one shared API key per node is coarse,

114
00:08:03,874 --> 00:08:08,934
write calls wait about half a second for finality, list endpoints have

115
00:08:08,984 --> 00:08:12,804
no pagination yet, and I measured on a single laptop.

116
00:08:13,554 --> 00:08:18,426
To conclude, I built a complete REST integration for a Corda CorDapp.

117
00:08:18,476 --> 00:08:23,348
A contract and four flows protect the invoice lifecycle, and a Spring

118
00:08:23,398 --> 00:08:28,341
Boot gateway turns eleven JSON endpoints into flows and vault queries.

119
00:08:28,391 --> 00:08:32,264
All forty two tests pass, and a Python ERP ran the full

120
00:08:32,314 --> 00:08:34,262
lifecycle on a live network.

121
00:08:34,312 --> 00:08:39,540
In future, I would add OAuth2, webhooks or Kafka events, idempotency keys,

122
00:08:39,590 --> 00:08:41,680
pagination and Docker Compose.

123
00:08:42,430 --> 00:08:44,421
That brings me to the end.

124
00:08:44,471 --> 00:08:49,603
All the code, tests, evidence and the Postman collection are in my

125
00:08:49,653 --> 00:08:56,041
public GitHub repository, corda rest api integration, under the account Momo-1628.

126
00:08:56,091 --> 00:09:02,165
My main references were the Corda 4.12 documentation, the R3 CorDapp template,

127
00:09:02,215 --> 00:09:05,306
the Spring Boot reference and springdoc.

128
00:09:05,356 --> 00:09:07,897
Thank you very much for watching.

129
00:09:07,947 --> 00:09:13,628
I am Suraj Patel, USN 24BTRBT029, and I am happy to answer any questions.
